// Target is the seam between pack and a system it installs on — Docker engines, Kubernetes, hosts reached by
// Ansible. pack knows no target: it renders a pack to a target-neutral release, plans the saga and drives it step
// by step; a target, a program of its own with its own go.mod and main, says what the environment's placement of a
// key means on its system and performs each step in it.
//
// pack starts the target — a binary (pack-target-<name>), or a container image run with podman or docker for a
// target whose language's runtime the machine lacks — and tells it in its environment where to listen and the
// token to demand:
//
//   PACK_TARGET_ADDRESS  unix:///<path> (a binary: a socket in a private directory) or tcp://<host>:<port> (an
//                        image: the port is published on 127.0.0.1 only)
//   PACK_TARGET_TOKEN    every call carries it as the metadata pack-target-token; refuse a call without it
//
